* Test CREAD parallel broadcast safety under -a RESIZE.
*

! Background: cread (source/io/coorio.F90:1974) broadcasts X, Y, Z,
! WMAIN to non-master ranks with PSND8(X, NATOM) etc, without calling
! resize_coord first.  Under -a RESIZE the coord arrays are initially
! allocated at size i0=1 in allocate_coord_ltm.  In practice the
! PSF-read / GENERATE pipeline grows them via resize_coord(NATOM)
! before any "read coor card" hits cread, so the broadcast lands in
! a properly-sized buffer.  This test confirms that contract: it
! builds a ~300-atom system, writes a CRD, reads it back, and
! asserts that every rank sees the right NATOM and bit-identical
! coordinates after the broadcast.  If a future refactor lets a
! "read coor card" hit cread with an undersized receive buffer on a
! non-master rank, the PSND8 inside cread will write past the end of
! X.  Under -a RESIZE + AddressSanitizer that surfaces as a
! heap-buffer-overflow at the PSND8 call site; under a plain build
! the corruption is silent until the next malloc, but the
! coordinate-comparison assertions below still trip on any per-rank
! corruption that affects the values themselves.

! The broadcast-overflow risk this test guards against only exists in
! parallel runs; PSNDC has an early return on NUMNOD==1.  We still run
! the @testcheck assertions in serial -- they validate the CRD
! write/read round-trip on the master, which is the precondition for
! the parallel-broadcast assertions to mean anything.

stream datadir.def

read rtf card name @0top_all22_prot.rtf
bomlev -2
read param card name @0par_all22_prot.prm
bomlev 0

! 30 ALA -> 303 atoms (10 atoms/residue plus terminal patches).  Large
! enough
! that any size-1 receive buffer overflows beyond the heap chunk
! boundary, small enough to run in a second.
read sequ ala 30
generate poly setup warn

! Build coordinates from IC table so we have something on disk to read.
ic param
ic seed 1 N 1 CA 1 C
ic build

@testcheck ?natom 303 -0.5 cread_initial_natom

! Sum-over-x for the whole system; used as a global fingerprint
! across ranks below.
coor stat sele all end
set xsumref ?xave

! Single-atom probe (atom 50 sits inside the chain, not on a terminus).
coor stat sele bynum 50 end
set x50 ?xave

! Master writes the CRD.  Receivers are quiet here.
open write card unit 30 name @9cread_resize.crd
write coor card unit 30
* CRD snapshot for cread_broadcast_resize test
*
close unit 30

! ---- The actual cread parallel broadcast under test ----
! Re-read the CRD we just wrote.  Inside cread the PSND8(X,NATOM)
! family of broadcasts fires on every rank.  If X/Y/Z on a non-master
! rank are sized < NATOM, this is where the heap overflow happens.
open read card unit 30 name @9cread_resize.crd
read coor card unit 30
close unit 30

! NATOM must be unchanged by the round-trip.
@testcheck ?natom 303 -0.5 cread_post_read_natom

! Atom 50 x-coordinate must round-trip identically.  ?xave is reduced
! across MPI ranks, so any per-rank corruption desynchronises the value.
coor stat sele bynum 50 end
@testcheck ?xave @x50 -0.0001 cread_atom50_x_roundtrip

! Sum-over-all-atoms x.  Catches any per-rank divergence in the X array
! that a single-atom probe might miss.
coor stat sele all end
@testcheck ?xave @xsumref -0.0001 cread_xsum_roundtrip

echo cread_broadcast_resize test failures: @testfail

stop
